1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,295
I'm going to give you a list in this lecture: categories of things these models are

2
00:00:05,295 --> 00:00:08,750
reliably good at, and categories they're reliably weak at.

3
00:00:08,750 --> 00:00:13,754
Before I do, I need to tell you something that makes the list more useful, not less.

4
00:00:13,754 --> 00:00:18,698
Some of what's in the "weak" column today will move to the "reliable" column within

5
00:00:18,698 --> 00:00:20,605
a year, sometimes within months.

6
00:00:20,605 --> 00:00:22,571
That's not a reason to skip this.

7
00:00:22,571 --> 00:00:23,881
It's the actual point.

8
00:00:23,881 --> 00:00:25,430
The specific list changes.

9
00:00:25,430 --> 00:00:29,839
The categories, and the discipline of testing rather than assuming, don't.

10
00:00:29,839 --> 00:00:34,545
By the end of this you'll have both: today's honest snapshot, and a five-minute

11
00:00:34,545 --> 00:00:38,238
method for re-checking it yourself whenever a new model ships.

12
00:00:38,238 --> 00:00:40,026
Most teams get this backwards.

13
00:00:40,026 --> 00:00:45,030
They either treat the model as capable of anything, because the demo was impressive,

14
00:00:45,030 --> 00:00:49,974
or they treat it as capable of nothing serious, because someone read a horror story

15
00:00:49,974 --> 00:00:50,272
once.

16
00:00:50,272 --> 00:00:52,595
Which one is your team doing right now?

17
00:00:52,595 --> 00:00:53,608
Both are guesses.

18
00:00:53,608 --> 00:00:57,063
The frontier is a specific, checkable line, not a feeling.

19
00:00:57,613 --> 00:01:02,623
Here's the current shape, in categories built to outlast any specific benchmark.

20
00:01:02,623 --> 00:01:06,130
Reliably strong: working with text you actually give it.

21
00:01:06,130 --> 00:01:11,139
Rewriting, summarising, pulling structured fields out of something unstructured,

22
00:01:11,139 --> 00:01:13,832
sorting things into categories you defined.

23
00:01:13,832 --> 00:01:18,841
This is pattern completion applied to material that's right there in the prompt,

24
00:01:18,841 --> 00:01:22,598
exactly where the mechanism from lecture one is at its best.

25
00:01:22,598 --> 00:01:27,608
Still genuinely risky: exact arithmetic, especially with longer numbers, because

26
00:01:27,608 --> 00:01:30,614
it's predicting digits as text, not calculating.

27
00:01:30,614 --> 00:01:35,248
Anything past its training cutoff, unless you hand it the answer yourself.

28
00:01:35,248 --> 00:01:40,508
And long chains of reasoning where nothing checks the middle steps, because an early

29
00:01:40,508 --> 00:01:44,828
small error compounds silently into a confidently wrong final answer.

30
00:01:44,828 --> 00:01:49,963
Notice the pattern connecting the whole right column: every one of these is a case

31
00:01:49,963 --> 00:01:55,098
where the mechanism has to extend past what's directly visible in the prompt, into

32
00:01:55,098 --> 00:01:59,669
genuine calculation, genuine recency, or genuine multi-step verification.

33
00:01:59,669 --> 00:02:02,988
That's precisely where pattern completion is weakest.

34
00:02:03,538 --> 00:02:08,520
Say the honest caveat, because pretending this list is permanent would be the least

35
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:10,801
defensible thing in this whole course.

36
00:02:10,801 --> 00:02:14,343
Every item in the risky column is being actively worked on.

37
00:02:14,343 --> 00:02:19,325
Arithmetic gets more reliable once a model can call an actual calculator instead of

38
00:02:19,325 --> 00:02:20,886
predicting digits as text.

39
00:02:20,886 --> 00:02:25,869
Some long reasoning chains get sturdier once a model can check its own intermediate

40
00:02:25,869 --> 00:02:27,970
steps rather than committing blind.

41
00:02:27,970 --> 00:02:32,952
Neither problem is solved everywhere, in every product, today, but both are visibly

42
00:02:32,952 --> 00:02:33,372
moving.

43
00:02:33,372 --> 00:02:37,034
That's exactly why the next slide matters more than this one.

44
00:02:37,034 --> 00:02:41,776
A team that memorised this table eighteen months ago and never checked again is

45
00:02:41,776 --> 00:02:46,758
making decisions on a map that's already partly wrong, in the specific direction of

46
00:02:46,758 --> 00:02:50,060
being too cautious about things that quietly got fixed.

47
00:02:50,610 --> 00:02:55,558
Here's the skill that actually survives every model upgrade, and it's the reason

48
00:02:55,558 --> 00:02:57,228
this section exists at all.

49
00:02:57,228 --> 00:03:02,053
Boundary probing means you don't take last year's capability list on faith, in

50
00:03:02,053 --> 00:03:03,104
either direction.

51
00:03:03,104 --> 00:03:08,238
You test, deliberately, at the edge of whatever category your feature sits in, with

52
00:03:08,238 --> 00:03:12,754
your own real cases, before you build a launch decision on an assumption.

53
00:03:12,754 --> 00:03:15,228
The exercise is that test, run for real.

54
00:03:15,228 --> 00:03:19,063
Whichever risky item is closest to your feature, try it today.

55
00:03:19,063 --> 00:03:24,135
Whatever you find is worth more than the table two slides back, because that table

56
00:03:24,135 --> 00:03:27,104
has an expiry date and your test result doesn't.

57
00:03:27,104 --> 00:03:32,299
This is also the entire reason Section four exists as a full section rather than one

58
00:03:32,299 --> 00:03:33,227
paragraph here.

59
00:03:33,227 --> 00:03:37,804
A boundary probe you run once, by hand, tells you where the edge is today.

60
00:03:37,804 --> 00:03:39,969
A golden set tells you if it moves.

61
00:03:40,519 --> 00:03:43,305
Here's what a real probe actually looks like.

62
00:03:43,305 --> 00:03:48,382
A support tool asked to count how many messages, out of a two hundred word thread,

63
00:03:48,382 --> 00:03:50,858
came from the customer versus the agent.

64
00:03:50,858 --> 00:03:52,716
Ten runs, exact same question.

65
00:03:52,716 --> 00:03:53,583
Three correct.

66
00:03:53,583 --> 00:03:58,102
And every single one of the ten, right or wrong, delivered with identical

67
00:03:58,102 --> 00:04:03,055
confidence, because that's the consequence from lecture one showing up again: no

68
00:04:03,055 --> 00:04:08,255
separate signal for "I'm not sure about this count." Counting is exactly the kind of

69
00:04:08,255 --> 00:04:12,094
task that looks trivial and sits squarely in the risky column.

70
00:04:12,094 --> 00:04:17,109
Nobody would have guessed three out of ten from the demo, because the demo used a

71
00:04:17,109 --> 00:04:22,185
five-message thread, and five is short enough to get right by pattern alone almost

72
00:04:22,185 --> 00:04:22,866
every time.

73
00:04:22,866 --> 00:04:26,395
The probe only found the gap because it pushed past that.

74
00:04:26,945 --> 00:04:28,740
One thing before you move on.

75
00:04:28,740 --> 00:04:33,568
Pick the risky item closest to what you're building, and put it to a real test

76
00:04:33,568 --> 00:04:37,900
against your own cases before the week is out, not a hypothetical one.

77
00:04:37,900 --> 00:04:39,509
Note down when you ran it.

78
00:04:39,509 --> 00:04:44,708
You'll want to know exactly how current that result is, the next time someone claims

79
00:04:44,708 --> 00:04:45,884
the frontier moved.

80
00:04:45,884 --> 00:04:50,278
Next lecture takes the risky column's most persistent member seriously:

81
00:04:50,278 --> 00:04:55,353
hallucination, and why it's the mechanism working as designed, not a bug anyone is

82
00:04:55,353 --> 00:04:56,529
going to patch out.
